abstract: >-
  Analytical codebase for a study quantifying how urban
  pluvial floods alter walking access to public and
  private not-for-profit (PNFP) healthcare facilities in
  Kampala, Uganda. The pipeline estimates population-weighted
  changes in travel time under 15 rainfall × duration
  scenarios for all facilities and hospitals separately.
  Inequities are quantified by intersecting flood-induced
  travel time losses with a nutrition-sensitive composite
  vulnerability index derived from socio-demographic,
  maternal and child health, and infectious disease
  indicators.
keywords:
  - pluvial flooding
  - healthcare accessibility
  - travel time modelling
  - AccessMod
  - Kampala
  - Uganda
  - vulnerability
  - maternal and child health
  - undernutrition
  - geospatial analysis
